/* Enriched answer frameworks, keyed by question id. Loaded after pyq_mains.js and merged in. */
window.MAINS_FW={
'm2016-2':{intro:'Art 149 lets Parliament prescribe the CAG\'s duties; the **CAG (DPC) Act 1971** does so. Audit has expanded from regularity to **performance** audit.',body:['**Mandate**: regularity (legal) audit is obligatory; **propriety** and **performance** audits examine economy, efficiency, effectiveness: CAG Auditing Standards (2017).','**Not overstepping**: the CAG evaluates whether a policy was **implemented** as Parliament intended; the PAC and Parliament decide on action. *Arvind Gupta v Union (2013)*: performance audit is within mandate; the CAG need not limit itself to accounts.','**Where criticism arises**: "presumptive loss" estimates (2G: ₹1.76 lakh crore; coal: ₹1.86 lakh crore) seen as policy second-guessing; audit of PPP and private entities; timing of reports.','**Safeguards**: CAG must not question **policy choice** itself, only its execution; follow auditing standards; consult the executive (exit conferences).'],conclusion:'Performance audit deepens accountability. Keeping the line between implementation and policy-making preserves legitimacy.',value:['**Arvind Gupta (2013)**','2G, Coal, CWG, Bharatmala (2023) audits','**INTOSAI** standards']},
'm2016-4':{intro:'Art 370 sat in Part XXI as a "temporary provision", drafted by **N. Gopalaswami Ayyangar** after J&K\'s accession (1947).',body:['**Why temporary**: war conditions, pending plebiscite idea, and the J&K Constituent Assembly\'s role under Art 370(3).','**Why it seemed permanent**: the Constituent Assembly dissolved in 1957 without recommending abrogation; *Sampat Prakash (1969)* and later cases treated it as continuing.','**Gradual erosion**: 40+ Presidential Orders extended most of the Constitution (1954 order, Art 35A).','**2019**: C.O. 272 and 273 made it inoperative; J&K Reorganisation Act created two UTs.','**2023**: *In Re Art 370*: temporary provision; President\'s power survived; C.O. 273 valid; statehood to be restored; elections by Sept 2024.'],conclusion:'The "temporary" label proved decisive. Future questions concern restoring statehood and democratic participation.',value:['**In Re Art 370 (2023)**','**Prem Nath Kaul (1959)**','**Sampat Prakash (1969)**']},
'm2016-5':{intro:'The **GoI Act 1935** (321 sections, 10 schedules) proposed an **All-India Federation** of provinces and princely states.',body:['**Federal features**: three lists (Federal 59, Provincial 54, Concurrent 36), provincial autonomy from 1937, **Federal Court** (1937), bicameral federal legislature, RBI.','**Unitary/non-federal features**: federation depended on princely states acceding (they never did); **residuary powers with the Governor-General**; GG\'s special responsibilities and discretion; dyarchy at the Centre never implemented; British Parliament retained supremacy.','**Legacy**: about 250 provisions of the 1950 Constitution draw on it; the emergency scheme, Governor\'s role and lists come from it.'],conclusion:'It laid down a federal blueprint without a functioning federation: "a machine with strong brakes but no engine" (Nehru).',value:['Nehru\'s quote','Simon Commission → RTCs → White Paper (1933)']},
'm2017-1':{intro:'India held simultaneous polls from **1951–52 to 1967**; the cycle broke due to premature dissolutions.',body:['**Gains**: cost (₹1 lakh crore+ across tiers), less MCC-induced policy paralysis, efficient deployment of forces and officials, higher turnout, less voter fatigue.','**Accountability risks**: fewer occasions for voters to judge governments; national issues may drown out State issues (studies show a higher chance of voting for the same party when polls coincide); fixed terms dilute the confidence principle.','**Design questions**: what happens on no-confidence or hung House (Kovind: elections only for the unexpired term); federalism and the basic structure; logistics (EVMs, VVPATs).','**Status**: Kovind Committee (2024) → **129th Amendment Bill (Dec 2024)** with a JPC.'],conclusion:'Simultaneous polls can save time and money if paired with safeguards such as a constructive vote of no-confidence and strong State-level accountability.',value:['**Law Commission 170th (1999), draft (2018)**','**Kovind Committee (2024)**','**Parliamentary Standing Committee 79th Report (2015)**']},
'm2017-2':{intro:'*K.S. Puttaswamy v Union (2017, 9 judges)* unanimously held that privacy is a fundamental right under **Art 21** and Part III.',body:['**Doctrinal shift**: overruled *M.P. Sharma (1954)* and *Kharak Singh (1962)*; privacy covers bodily, informational and decisional autonomy.','**Tests for restriction**: (1) legality (a law), (2) legitimate aim, (3) **proportionality**, (4) procedural safeguards (Justice Kaul).','**Expansion of FRs**: *Navtej Johar (2018)*: Sec 377; *Joseph Shine (2018)*: adultery; *Aadhaar (2018)*: Sec 57 struck; *Common Cause (2018)*: living wills; *X v Principal Secretary (2022)*: reproductive autonomy; *Anuradha Bhasin (2020)*.','**Horizontal and data dimensions**: need for a data protection law → **DPDP Act 2023**; surveillance questions (Pegasus committee, 2021).'],conclusion:'Puttaswamy made dignity and autonomy the centre of Part III. Its promise depends on strong data protection and judicial scrutiny of surveillance.',value:['**Puttaswamy (2017)**','**Justice B.N. Srikrishna Committee (2018)**','**DPDP Act 2023**']},
'm2017-3':{intro:'The **PAC** (1921, under the GoI Act 1919) examines the **CAG\'s audit reports** on Union accounts. It has 22 members (15 LS + 7 RS) and an Opposition chairman by convention since 1967.',body:['**Accountability role**: checks that money was spent legally and for the purpose granted; examines irregularities, waste and losses; Action Taken Reports from ministries.','**Impact**: 2G spectrum, Commonwealth Games, coal block allocations, Bofors; created public pressure and follow-up action.','**Limitations**: post-mortem; recommendations advisory; cannot examine policy; huge backlog of CAG paras; politicisation (2G draft report controversy, 2011).','**Strengthening**: time-bound ATRs, research staff, real-time audit, public hearings.'],conclusion:'The PAC is the key link in the chain: Executive → CAG → PAC → Parliament → People.',value:['**CAG**: "friend, philosopher and guide"','PAC reports on 2G and CWG']},
'm2017-4':{intro:'The **99th Amendment** and the **NJAC Act 2014** replaced the collegium; struck down by a 5-judge bench (**4:1**) in Oct 2015.',body:['**Majority reasoning**: judicial **primacy** in appointments is part of judicial independence, a basic feature; the Law Minister and two eminent persons (any two could veto) could compromise independence.','**Justice Chelameswar\'s dissent**: collegium lacks transparency; executive participation is not unconstitutional.','**After**: court admitted collegium flaws; directed a revised **Memorandum of Procedure** (still not finalised); collegium publishes resolutions since 2017.','**Ongoing issues**: delays, government sitting on recommendations, lack of diversity, opacity.'],conclusion:'Neither pure collegium nor executive-led commission is ideal. A transparent, broad-based body with judicial majority and published criteria could balance independence and accountability.',value:['**Second Judges (1993)**, **Third Judges (1998)**, **Fourth Judges (2015)**','**NCRWC (2002)**: National Judicial Commission']},
'm2018-2':{intro:'Committees are Parliament\'s "mini-legislatures": they allow detailed, bipartisan and expert scrutiny that the floor cannot.',body:['**Usefulness**: time (Parliament sits ~60 days a year), expertise (witnesses, experts), consensus across parties, continuous oversight, scrutiny of Bills and Budgets (DRSCs since 1993).','**Estimates Committee**: 30 LS members, chairman from the ruling party; examines estimates, suggests alternative policies for economy and efficiency; "continuous economy committee".','**Limitations**: examines after voting; cannot question policy laid down by Parliament; advisory; covers few ministries a year; no CAG support.','**Trend**: fewer Bills referred to committees (17th LS: ~16%).'],conclusion:'Mandatory referral of Bills, research support and follow-up mechanisms would make committees more effective.',value:['PRS data on referral','**NCRWC**','**John Mathai** (first Estimates Committee, 1950)']},
'm2018-3':{intro:'The **73rd Amendment (1992)** created a constitutional third tier: Gram Sabha, three-tier panchayats, SECs and SFCs.',body:['**Importance**: participatory democracy; planning from below (GPDP); women\'s leadership (1/3, 50% in 20+ States); social justice through SC/ST reservation; service delivery (water, sanitation, roads).','**Own-source revenue (tiny: <10%)**: property and house tax, market fees, water charges, licences, tolls, leasing of common property (ponds, fisheries), professional tax.','**Other sources**: CSR funds, MPLADS/MLALADS convergence, PPP for markets, community contributions, cooperatives, crowdfunding, carbon credits from social forestry, loans through State-level bodies.','**Enablers**: SFC implementation, devolution of the 3Fs, eGramSwaraj, capacity building (RGSA).'],conclusion:'Fiscal autonomy is the missing link. Panchayats need a stronger own-revenue base and predictable transfers.',value:['**15th FC**: ₹2.36 lakh crore','**2nd ARC 6th Report**','**SVAMITVA** property cards for tax base']},
'm2018-4':{intro:'EVMs were used nationally from 2004; VVPAT since 2013. Controversies peaked around 2017–19.',body:['**Challenges**: trust deficit and misinformation; allegations of tampering; VVPAT verification only on a sample; transparency of source code; technical glitches; deepfakes and social media manipulation; money power; voter roll errors.','**ECI steps**: VVPAT in all seats (2019); 5 VVPATs per Assembly segment (*N. Chandrababu Naidu, 2019*); hackathon challenge (2017); randomisation and FLC; mock polls; candidate agents\' participation.','**SC**: *ADR v ECI (2024)* rejected paper ballots and 100% VVPAT count; allowed burnt-memory checks of 5% EVMs on request.','**Further**: independent audits, statistically sound VVPAT sampling, voter education.'],conclusion:'Trust in elections rests on procedure, transparency and communication, not just technology.',value:['**ADR v ECI (2024)**','**Subramanian Swamy (2013)**: VVPAT']},
'm2018-5':{intro:'*GNCTD v Union (July 2018, 5 judges)* interpreted Art 239AA.',body:['**Holding**: the LG is bound by **aid and advice** except on public order, police and land; "any matter" in Art 239AA(4) means exceptional matters, not every issue; no independent decision-making power; constitutional morality and collaborative federalism.','**What it settled**: LG not to be an obstructionist; decisions to be communicated, not need concurrence.','**What it did not**: control of **services** was left to a regular bench; split verdict (2019); a 5-judge bench (May 2023) gave services to the elected government.','**Legislative response**: GNCTD (Amendment) Acts **2021** and **2023** (NCCSA; LG\'s final say).'],conclusion:'The judgment narrowed the conflict but did not end it. Durable peace needs political conventions and clarity on Delhi\'s status.',value:['**GNCTD 2018, 2023**','**Art 239AA(4)**']},
'm2018-6':{intro:'Art 280: the President constitutes a Finance Commission every five years.',body:['**Composition**: chairman + 4 members; qualifications by the FC (Misc. Provisions) Act 1951.','**Functions**: vertical and horizontal devolution, grants-in-aid (Art 275), measures to augment local body resources, other matters referred.','**15th FC ToR** (2017): 2011 census, incentives for population control, performance-based incentives (GST, DBT, power sector), defence and internal security funding, revenue deficit grants.','**Outcome**: 41% (2020–26); demographic performance criterion; local body grants; disaster windows; Modernisation Fund for Defence.','**16th FC (2026–31)**: 41% retained; GDP contribution criterion.'],conclusion:'The FC remains the balancing wheel, but cesses, surcharges and conditional grants need to be addressed.',value:['**14th (42%)**, **15th (41%)**, **16th (41%)**']},
'm2018-7':{intro:'The NCSC (Art 338) investigates and monitors safeguards for SCs; minority institutions enjoy autonomy under **Art 30**.',body:['**NCSC powers**: civil court powers, investigation and recommendation; cannot compel.','**Constitutional position**: **Art 15(5)** excludes **minority educational institutions** from reservation; *Pramati (2014)* upheld; religious minority institutions can admit their community.','**Employment**: Art 16(4) reservation not mandatory for minority institutions; Art 16(5) protects religious institutions.','**Role**: NCSC can investigate discrimination against SC individuals, advise governments, and highlight issues; cannot enforce reservation against Art 30 rights.'],conclusion:'NCSC cannot enforce constitutional reservation in religious minority institutions; it can only investigate and recommend.',value:['**Art 15(5)**','**Pramati (2014)**','**T.M.A. Pai (2002)**']},
'm2019-1':{intro:'Art 368 empowers Parliament to amend "by way of addition, variation or repeal". The question is whether this power is unlimited.',body:['**Kesavananda (1973)**: Parliament can amend any part but not the **basic structure**.','**42nd Amendment (1976)**: Art 368(4),(5) tried to make the amending power unlimited and non-justiciable.','**Minerva Mills (1980)**: struck them down. "Parliament cannot, under Art 368, expand its amending power so as to acquire the power to repeal or abrogate the Constitution." **Limited amending power** is itself basic.','**Later**: *Waman Rao (1981)*, *I.R. Coelho (2007)*, *NJAC (2015)*.'],conclusion:'Parliament cannot destroy the basic structure, nor expand its own power to do so. The doctrine preserves constitutional identity.',value:['**Minerva Mills (1980)**','**Kesavananda (1973)**']},
'm2019-2':{intro:'Disputes over legislative competence under **Art 246** and the Seventh Schedule have produced interpretive doctrines.',body:['**Federal supremacy**: in a conflict, the **Union List** prevails over State and Concurrent Lists; Concurrent over State (Art 246(1)–(3)); Central law prevails on Concurrent subjects (Art 254), unless a reserved State law got assent.','**Harmonious construction**: read entries so that each has effect and conflict is avoided; only if irreconcilable does federal supremacy apply.','**Pith and substance**: true nature of the law (*F.N. Balsara, 1951*). **Colourable legislation**, **incidental encroachment**.','**Recent**: *MADA v SAIL (2024)*: State taxing power on mineral rights read harmoniously with MMDR Act; *Jindal Stainless (2016)*.'],conclusion:'These doctrines help courts uphold federal balance without paralysing either level of government.',value:['**F.N. Balsara (1951)**','**MADA v SAIL (2024)**']},
'm2019-3':{intro:'France follows **laïcité**: strict separation; India follows **positive secularism**: equal respect and principled distance (Rajeev Bhargava).',body:['**Indian features**: no State religion; Arts 25–28 freedom; Arts 29–30 minority rights; State can reform religious practices (Art 25(2)); aid to religious institutions without discrimination.','**France**: headscarf ban in schools (2004), burqa ban (2010), abaya ban (2023); assimilationist.','**Lessons**: accommodation of visible religious identity; community rights; contextual approach builds inclusion.','**Caveats**: Indian secularism faces communalism; *S.R. Bommai* made secularism a basic feature.'],conclusion:'India\'s model of equal respect offers France a way to reconcile neutrality with pluralism.',value:['**S.R. Bommai (1994)**','Rajeev Bhargava: "principled distance"']},
'm2019-4':{intro:'Separation of powers is part of the **basic structure** (*Indira Gandhi, 1975*) but the Constitution does not adopt it rigidly.',body:['**Overlaps**: executive drawn from legislature (Arts 74, 75); President\'s ordinances (Art 123); Parliament\'s judicial functions (impeachment, privilege); executive\'s role in judicial appointments; judiciary\'s rule-making.','**Checks and balances**: judicial review (Art 13, 32, 226); legislative control (questions, no-confidence, PAC); executive veto and pardon; Art 50 separation of judiciary.','**Judicial view**: *Ram Jawaya Kapur (1955)*: no rigid separation; *Kesavananda*: separation is basic.','**Recent**: Governor timelines case (2025): advisory opinion said courts cannot fix timelines, a check on judicial overreach.'],conclusion:'India follows functional separation with checks and balances rather than watertight compartments.',value:['**Ram Jawaya Kapur (1955)**','**Indira Gandhi v Raj Narain (1975)**']},
'm2019-5':{intro:'The **CAT** was set up in 1985 under **Art 323A** and the Administrative Tribunals Act.',body:['**Original intent**: speedy, cheap, specialised resolution of service disputes; relieve HCs.','**Independent judicial authority**: judicial and administrative members; powers of a civil court; follows natural justice; can examine constitutional validity of rules (except its parent Act).','**L. Chandra Kumar (1997)**: CAT is a court of first instance; its orders go to a HC division bench.','**Issues**: executive control over appointments (sponsoring ministry), vacancies, pendency.'],conclusion:'CAT now functions as a quasi-judicial court; its independence needs institutional support (National Tribunals Commission, 2026).',value:['**L. Chandra Kumar (1997)**','**Tribunals Reforms Act 2026**']},
'm2019-6':{intro:'MPs are meant to legislate, deliberate and hold the executive to account.',body:['**Decline**: anti-defection law and whips; fewer sittings (17th LS ~55 days/year); disruptions; Bills passed without debate or committee scrutiny; ordinances; delegated legislation.','**Quality impact**: farm laws (2020) passed by voice vote; criminal law Bills (2023) after mass suspensions; low private members\' Bill success.','**Other factors**: criminalisation, money in politics, party centralisation, lack of research support.','**Reforms**: limit whips, fixed calendar, Opposition days, research staff (LAMP fellows model), mandatory committee referral.'],conclusion:'Reviving the individual MP\'s role is central to the health of parliamentary democracy.',value:['PRS data','**NCRWC**']},
'm2019-7':{intro:'RPA 1951 lays down disqualifications in Secs 8–11.',body:['**Grounds**: conviction (Sec 8: specified offences, 6 months+ for certain, 2 years+ any offence); corrupt practices (Sec 8A); dismissal for corruption or disloyalty (Sec 9); government contracts (Sec 9A); failure to lodge election expenses (Sec 10A); office of profit and others under Art 102.','**Lily Thomas (2013)**: immediate disqualification on conviction.','**Remedies**: appeal and **stay of conviction** (Rahul Gandhi, 2023); **ECI** can remove or reduce disqualification (**Sec 11**, P.S. Golay 2019); election petition; writ.'],conclusion:'Clear grounds and remedies balance probity with fairness.',value:['**Lily Thomas (2013)**','**Lok Prahari (2018)**']},
'm2019-8':{intro:'The 73rd/74th Amendments reserved 1/3 seats (50% in 20+ States) for women.',body:['**Gains**: over 14 lakh elected women representatives; agenda shifts to water, health, education (Chattopadhyay–Duflo study); role models.','**Limits**: proxy rule ("sarpanch-pati"), rotation reduces experience, caste and patriarchy, low capacity, violence and harassment.','**Spillover**: few women move to State and national politics; women\'s reservation in legislatures came only in 2023.','**Remedies**: training, longer rotation cycles, penalties for proxies (MoPR 2023–25), women\'s sabhas.'],conclusion:'Reservation opened the door but has not dismantled patriarchy; capacity and social change are needed.',value:['**106th Amendment**','MoPR advisory committee (2023)']},
'm2020-1':{intro:'The Constitution\'s unitary bias (strong Centre) aims at unity and integrity.',body:['**Epidemic Diseases Act 1897**: colonial law used during COVID-19; amended 2020 to protect health workers.','**DM Act 2005**: NDMA/NEC orders bound States during lockdowns; Centre-led response.','**Farm Acts (2020)**: Centre legislated on trade and commerce (Concurrent entry 33) touching agriculture (State List); protests; repealed 2021.','**Constitutional basis**: Arts 246, 249–253, 256, 257, 355; Union List residuary power.','**Concerns**: consultation deficit; federal friction.'],conclusion:'Centralisation can deliver speed in crises, but legitimacy needs consultation (ISC, GST Council model).',value:['**DM Act 2005**','**Mohit Minerals**']},
'm2020-2':{intro:'Judicial legislation means courts laying down rules where the legislature has not.',body:['**Concern**: separation of powers; unelected judges; institutional competence.','**Justification of PIL guidelines**: legislative vacuum (*Vishaka* 1997 until POSH Act 2013), FR protection (*D.K. Basu* 1997), institutional failures (*Prakash Singh* 2006, *Vineet Narain* 1998).','**Guardrails**: guidelines are interim; legislatures can replace them; courts self-restrain (*Balwant Singh Chaufal* 2010).'],conclusion:'Filling vacuums to protect rights is justified; permanent policy-making by courts is not.',value:['**Vishaka (1997)**','**Prakash Singh (2006)**']},
'm2020-3':{intro:'In the UK, the Speaker resigns from party and is usually re-elected unopposed; India has no such convention.',body:['**Benefits**: neutrality in Tenth Schedule decisions, Money Bill certification, conduct of House.','**Indian concerns**: Speakers deciding defection cases with delay or partisanship (*Keisham Meghachandra 2020*).','**Implications**: needs cross-party consensus on not contesting the Speaker\'s seat; loss of representation for that constituency.'],conclusion:'Adopt with consensus and complementary reforms (independent tribunal for defections).',value:['**Kihoto Hollohan**','**Keisham Meghachandra (2020)**']},
'm2020-4':{intro:'Indian federalism shows co-operative, competitive and confrontational strands.',body:['**Co-operation**: GST Council consensus, COVID vaccination, NITI Governing Council.','**Competition**: NITI indices, Ease of Doing Business rankings, investment summits.','**Confrontation**: Governor–State disputes (TN, Kerala, Punjab), CBI consent withdrawal, GST compensation, borrowing limits, Farm Acts, deployment of forces.'],conclusion:'Federalism is a negotiation; institutional fora must handle disagreements.',value:['**Mohit Minerals (2022)**','**Presidential Reference (2025)**']},
'm2020-5':{intro:'The RS was once dismissed as a secondary chamber.',body:['**Factors**: coalition era (ruling side often in minority in RS), regional parties, more expert members, committee work.','**Areas**: revising Bills (Land Acquisition Ordinance 2015 stalled), detailed debates, representing States, Art 249 and 312 powers, DRSC chairmanships.','**Limits**: Money Bill route to bypass RS (Aadhaar 2016).'],conclusion:'The RS has become a revising chamber of real value.',value:['**Rojer Mathew (2019)**']},
'm2020-6':{intro:'Constitutional status requires an **Art 368** amendment (e.g. NCBC via the 102nd).',body:['**Steps**: Bill with special majority; possibly State ratification if federal provisions are affected; define composition, functions, powers.','**NCW now**: statutory (1990 Act), recommendatory, civil court powers, limited resources.','**Would status help?** More permanence and prestige; but the problem is lack of enforcement powers, resources, and independent appointments.'],conclusion:'Constitutional status alone will not ensure justice; powers, independence and implementation matter.',value:['**102nd Amendment**','**NCW Act 1990**']},
'm2020-7':{intro:'Sec 8A RPA: disqualification for corrupt practices is decided by the President on the ECI\'s opinion after an HC finding.',body:['**Problem**: long election petitions, appeal delays; disqualification may come after the term ends.','**Simplification**: time-bound trials (6 months as per Sec 86(7)), special benches, direct ECI role, automatic disqualification on HC finding with stay possibility.'],conclusion:'Speed is essential for deterrence.',value:['**Law Commission 244th, 255th**']},
'm2021-1':{intro:'**Constitutional morality**: adherence to the core values of the Constitution (dignity, equality, liberty, fraternity) over popular morality.',body:['**Origin**: Ambedkar (citing Grote): it must be cultivated.','**Cases**: *Manoj Narula (2014)*; *Navtej Johar (2018)*; *Sabarimala (2018)*; *Joseph Shine (2018)*; *GNCTD (2018)*.','**Critique**: vague; risk of judicial subjectivity.'],conclusion:'A guide to interpretation that must be used with restraint.',value:['**Navtej (2018)**','Ambedkar, 4 Nov 1948']},
'm2021-2':{intro:'Both constitutions guarantee equality but differ in approach.',body:['**USA**: 14th Amendment equal protection; formal equality; affirmative action restricted (*SFFA v Harvard, 2023*).','**India**: Art 14 (formal + substantive), Arts 15–17 (special provisions, untouchability), reservation (Arts 15(4), 16(4)), EWS (103rd).','**Doctrines**: reasonable classification, arbitrariness, creamy layer, sub-classification (2024).'],conclusion:'India embraces substantive equality more explicitly.',value:['**Indra Sawhney**','**Davinder Singh (2024)**']},
'm2021-3':{intro:'Art 168 and **Art 169**: Parliament can create or abolish a Council on an Assembly special-majority resolution.',body:['**Composition**: Art 171.','**Status**: 6 States (AP, Bihar, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana, UP).','**Working**: revising role weak; used for accommodating leaders.','**Illustrations**: AP abolished and revived; TN abolished; pending proposals of WB, Rajasthan, Assam, Odisha.'],conclusion:'A national policy on second chambers would end ad hocism.',value:['**Art 169**']},
'm2021-4':{intro:'24 DRSCs since 1993; each 31 members.',body:['**Strengths**: scrutiny of Demands for Grants and Bills; expert inputs (e.g. Data Protection Bill review).','**Weaknesses**: falling referrals; 1-year term; recommendations advisory; attendance issues.','**Examples**: committee reports on GST, Cyber Security.'],conclusion:'Mandatory referral and longer tenures would strengthen them.',value:['PRS']},
'm2021-5':{intro:'Parliamentary accountability is the core of the Westminster model.',body:['**Tools**: questions, Zero Hour, motions, debates, committees, CAG via PAC, budget control.','**Limits**: executive dominance, majoritarian governments, few sittings, disruptions, ordinances.','**Remedies**: Opposition days, PM\'s Question Time, strengthened committees.'],conclusion:'Accountability is partial; reforms needed.',value:['**NCRWC**']},
'm2021-6':{intro:'Women are under-represented in the higher judiciary.',body:['**Data**: only a handful of women SC judges ever; ~14% in HCs.','**Reasons**: collegium opacity, pipeline issues.','**Benefits**: diverse perspectives, public trust.','**Steps**: Justice Nagarathna likely CJI in 2027.'],conclusion:'Diversity strengthens justice.',value:['**Collegium**']},
'm2021-7':{intro:'Civil society and NGOs deliver services alongside the State.',body:['**Models**: SEWA, Pratham, Akshaya Patra, Kudumbashree.','**Challenges**: scale, funding (FCRA 2020), accountability, sustainability.'],conclusion:'They complement, not replace, the State.',value:['**FCRA 2020**']},
'm2022-1':{intro:'**Art 64**: VP is ex officio Chairman of the RS.',body:['**Role**: presides, maintains order, casting vote, decides privilege, chairs committees.','**Neutrality**: Chairman not a member; removal only as VP.','**Recent**: 2024 removal notice; 2025 resignation.'],conclusion:'Impartiality is central.',value:['**Art 64, 67**']},
'm2022-2':{intro:'**RPA Part VI**: election disputes.',body:['**Procedure**: petition in HC within 45 days; trial within 6 months; appeal to SC.','**Grounds (Sec 100)**: disqualification, corrupt practice, improper acceptance or rejection of nomination, non-compliance with law.','**Cases**: *Indira Gandhi v Raj Narain (1975)*; *Abhiram Singh (2017)*.'],conclusion:'Speedier disposal needed.',value:['**Abhiram Singh (2017)**']},
'm2022-3':{intro:'*Puttaswamy (2017)*: privacy is intrinsic to Art 21.',body:['**DNA testing**: *Bhabani Prasad Jena (2010)*: DNA tests only when necessary; *Ashok Kumar (2021)*: privacy considerations.','**Balance**: child\'s rights, mother\'s privacy.'],conclusion:'Courts balance privacy with justice.',value:['**Puttaswamy**']},
'm2022-4':{intro:'**Art 213**: Governor\'s ordinance power.',body:['**Conditions**: legislature not in session; immediate action; President\'s instructions in certain cases.','**Re-promulgation**: *D.C. Wadhwa (1987)*, *Krishna Kumar Singh (2017)*: fraud on the Constitution.'],conclusion:'Re-promulgation is unconstitutional.',value:['**Krishna Kumar Singh (2017)**']},
'm2022-5':{intro:'Environmental rights through Art 21.',body:['**Cases**: M.C. Mehta (absolute liability), Godavarman, Vellore Citizens (precautionary principle), Ranjitsinh (2024).'],conclusion:'Courts have shaped environmental law.',value:['**M.K. Ranjitsinh (2024)**']},
'm2022-6':{intro:'India: indirect election; France: direct.',body:['**India**: electoral college, STV, vote value.','**France**: direct, two-round system.'],conclusion:'Different logics.',value:['**Art 54, 55**']},
'm2023-1':{intro:'Britain follows **parliamentary sovereignty** (Dicey); India follows **constitutional supremacy**.',body:['**UK**: no written constitution; courts cannot strike down Acts; declarations of incompatibility (HRA 1998).','**India**: written constitution; judicial review (Art 13, 32); basic structure; federalism.','**Convergence**: UK Supreme Court (2009), *Miller* cases; India\'s parliamentary practices from UK.'],conclusion:'India blends parliamentary government with judicial review.',value:['**Kesavananda**','**R (Miller) v PM (2019)**']},
'm2023-2':{intro:'Committees are standing or ad hoc.',body:['**Financial**: PAC, Estimates, COPU.','**Institutionalisation**: scrutiny, accountability, expertise.','**Limits**: post-mortem.'],conclusion:'Strengthen financial committees.',value:['PAC on 2G']},
'm2023-3':{intro:'Art 356 use fell after the mid-1990s.',body:['**Legal**: *S.R. Bommai (1994)*: justiciable, floor test.','**Political**: coalitions, regional parties, public opinion.'],conclusion:'Bommai changed practice.',value:['**S.R. Bommai**']},
'm2023-4':{intro:'101st Amendment (2016) introduced GST.',body:['**Significance**: one nation one tax; Art 246A, 269A, 279A.','**Federalism**: GST Council co-operative; *Mohit Minerals*.','**Concerns**: compensation, Centre\'s veto.'],conclusion:'Accommodative but centralising.',value:['**Mohit Minerals (2022)**']},
'm2023-5':{intro:'Presiding officers ensure order and impartiality.',body:['**Role**: rulings, decorum, Tenth Schedule.','**Concerns**: partisanship.','**Cases**: *Subhash Desai (2023)*.'],conclusion:'Neutrality must be ensured.',value:['**Subhash Desai (2023)**']},
'm2023-6':{intro:'Judicial independence is a prerequisite of democracy.',body:['**Safeguards**: tenure, salary, collegium, contempt.','**Threats**: appointments delays, post-retirement jobs.'],conclusion:'Essential for rule of law.',value:['**NJAC case**']},
'm2023-7':{intro:'**Art 39A** and the LSA Act 1987.',body:['**Eligible**: SC/ST, women, children, PwDs, custody, low-income.','**NALSA role**: Lok Adalats, LADCS, legal literacy.'],conclusion:'Expand reach.',value:['**NALSA**']},
'm2024-1':{intro:'The Constitution evolves through interpretation.',body:['**Art 21**: Maneka, Puttaswamy, Ranjitsinh (2024), shelter (2024).'],conclusion:'Art 21 shows dynamism.',value:['**Maneka Gandhi**']},
'm2024-2':{intro:'Electoral reforms debated by many committees.',body:['**Committees**: Goswami, Indrajit Gupta, Law Commission, Kovind.','**ONOE**: Kovind 2024; 129th Bill.'],conclusion:'Balanced reforms.',value:['**Kovind (2024)**']},
'm2024-3':{intro:'Centre–State relations have seen changes.',body:['**Changes**: GST, cesses, CBI consent, Governors, borrowing.'],conclusion:'Trust-building.',value:['**Punchhi**']},
'm2024-4':{intro:'The CAG audits legality and propriety.',body:['**Types**: legal, propriety, performance audit.'],conclusion:'Broader accountability.',value:['**CAG Act 1971**']},
'm2025-3':{intro:'J&K Assembly under the 2019 Act.',body:['**Nature**: UT legislature like Puducherry; 90 elected; LG nominations.','**Powers**: State and Concurrent List except public order and police; LG\'s approval for many matters (2024 rules).'],conclusion:'Limited powers; statehood pending.',value:['**In Re Art 370**']},
'm2025-4':{intro:'**Art 76**: AG.',body:['**Duties**: advice, appear in SC, Art 143 references.','**Rights**: audience, speak in Parliament.','**Limits**: no briefs against GoI, private practice limits.'],conclusion:'Independence matters.',value:['**Art 76, 88, 105**']},
'm2025-5':{intro:'Arts 323A, 323B.',body:['**Need**: specialisation, speed.','**2021 Act**: abolished tribunals; struck down partly.','**2026 Act**: National Tribunals Commission.'],conclusion:'Independence key.',value:['**MBA (2021)**']},
'm2025-6':{intro:'Art 368 has procedural and substantive limits.',body:['**Procedural**: special majority, ratification.','**Substantive**: basic structure.'],conclusion:'Balanced.',value:['**Kesavananda**']},
'm2025-7':{intro:'Fiscal federalism evolved through planning.',body:['**Phases**: Planning Commission, NITI, GST, FCs.'],conclusion:'Mixed.',value:['**16th FC**']},
};
Object.assign(window.MAINS_FW,{
'm2021-3':{intro:'**Art 168** provides for bicameral legislatures in some States; **Art 169** lets **Parliament**, by simple-majority law, create or abolish a Legislative Council if the State Assembly passes a resolution by a **special majority**. Art 171 fixes composition (max 1/3 of the Assembly, min 40).',body:['**Composition (Art 171)**: 1/3 by local bodies, 1/12 by graduates, 1/12 by teachers, 1/3 by MLAs, 1/6 nominated by the Governor: meant to bring expertise and indirect representation.','**Current status**: six States: **AP, Bihar, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana, UP**. J&K\'s Council abolished in 2019.','**Working**: can delay ordinary Bills only up to 4 months (Art 197); no say on Money Bills beyond 14 days; no joint sitting; often used to accommodate defeated or nominated leaders; graduate/teacher constituencies have low turnout.','**Illustrations**: AP abolished (1985), revived (2007), resolved to abolish (2020), withdrew (2021); TN abolished (1986), revival Act (2010) not implemented; WB (2021), Rajasthan (2012), Assam (2013), Odisha (2018) resolutions pending with Parliament.','**Case for**: deliberation, expert voices, check on hasty laws. **Case against**: cost, delay, patronage, dependence on the Assembly\'s will.'],conclusion:'The current ad hoc pattern (create, abolish, revive) undermines the institution. A national policy, perhaps through the Inter-State Council, on when States should have Councils would bring coherence.',value:['**Art 169, 171, 197**','Pending State resolutions','**NCRWC** view on second chambers']},
'm2021-4':{intro:'Seventeen DRSCs were set up in **1993** on the Rules Committee\'s recommendation, expanded to **24** in 2004 (16 LS, 8 RS). Each has 31 members; ministers cannot be members.',body:['**Keeping administration on its toes**: examine Demands for Grants of each ministry before voting; scrutinise Bills (e.g. Data Protection Bill reviews, the Consumer Protection Bill, Surrogacy Bill changes); annual reports; long-term policy documents; summon secretaries.','**Examples of impact**: Standing Committee on Finance changed the Insolvency and GST-related Bills; Home Affairs committee on criminal law Bills (2023) proposed changes, some accepted; committee on IT on digital privacy.','**Limits**: advisory; **1-year** tenure hampers expertise; falling referral of Bills (**60%** in 15th LS → **27%** in 16th → **16%** in 17th); attendance issues; no independent research staff; reports often ignored; no follow-up mechanism for Budget recommendations.','**Reverence for parliamentary control**: in-camera, non-partisan culture; but government\'s majority on committees and whip culture reduce independence.'],conclusion:'DRSCs work when Bills are referred and recommendations are tracked. Mandatory referral, two-year tenure and research support would restore their bite.',value:['PRS data on referrals','**2nd ARC**','**NCRWC**']},
'm2021-5':{intro:'In a parliamentary system, the executive is accountable to the legislature (Art 75(3)). Parliament\'s ability to enforce this is mixed.',body:['**Instruments**: Question Hour (starred/unstarred), Zero Hour, calling attention, adjournment and no-confidence motions, debates on the President\'s address, Budget and cut motions, committees (PAC, Estimates, DRSCs), CAG via PAC, assurances committee.','**Where it works**: PAC and CAG on 2G and coal; question-driven disclosures; no-confidence debates (2018, 2023) force government replies.','**Where it fails**: fewer sitting days (~55/year in the 17th LS); disruptions; Question Hour suspended (2020 Monsoon Session); Bills passed without debate; ordinances; Money Bill route; anti-defection whips; majoritarian dominance; vacant Deputy Speaker; low committee referral.','**Reforms**: fixed parliamentary calendar (NCRWC: 120 days for LS), Opposition days, PM\'s Question Time, mandatory committee referral, strengthened research, a Parliamentary Budget Office.'],conclusion:'Parliament retains formidable tools, but their use depends on political will and institutional design. Strengthening them is essential to keep the executive answerable.',value:['**NCRWC (2002)**','PRS data','**Rojer Mathew (2019)**']},
'm2021-6':{intro:'Women make up nearly half of India but are a small fraction of the higher judiciary: only a handful of women have ever served on the SC; about 13–14% of HC judges are women.',body:['**Why it matters**: legitimacy and public confidence; diverse life experience improves reasoning on gender, family, sexual violence; role models; equality under Arts 14–16.','**Barriers**: opaque collegium; pipeline issues at the Bar (few senior women advocates designated); long working hours and care burden; bias; age-based seniority.','**Progress**: three women judges sworn in together (2021); **Justice B.V. Nagarathna** likely to be the first woman CJI (2027); women majority among new judicial officers in several States.','**Measures**: diversity targets in MoP; transparent criteria; more senior designations for women; family-friendly infrastructure; data publication.'],conclusion:'Diversity is not tokenism; it strengthens justice. A conscious, transparent appointments process can accelerate it.',value:['**Indira Jaising (2017)** on senior designation','CJI Ramana\'s call for 50% representation (2021)']},
'm2021-7':{intro:'Civil society organisations (CSOs) and NGOs have long delivered services in health, education, livelihoods and relief, often where the State is weak.',body:['**Alternative models**: **SEWA** (informal workers), **Pratham** (learning outcomes, ASER), **Akshaya Patra** (mid-day meals PPP), **Kudumbashree** (SHG-State partnership), **Barefoot College**, Aravind Eye Care (low-cost model), **MKSS** (accountability).','**Advantages**: local knowledge, flexibility, innovation, trust, last-mile reach, cost efficiency.','**Challenges**: scale and sustainability; dependence on donors and CSR; **FCRA 2020** restrictions (no sub-granting, 20% admin cap); accountability and transparency gaps; uneven quality; politicisation; regulatory hostility; elite capture.','**Best fit**: partnership models (PPPs, social impact bonds, community monitoring) rather than substitution.'],conclusion:'NGOs complement but cannot replace the State\'s responsibility. A stable regulatory environment and outcome-based partnerships would maximise their value.',value:['**Noel Harper (2022)**','**National Policy on Voluntary Sector (2007)**','**NGO Darpan**']},
'm2022-1':{intro:'Under **Art 64** the Vice-President is ex officio **Chairman of the Rajya Sabha**; he is not a member of the House.',body:['**Presiding role**: conducts proceedings, decides points of order, maintains decorum, can suspend members, adjourns for lack of quorum.','**Powers**: casting vote only in a tie (Art 100); certifies nothing on Money Bills (the Speaker does); admits motions; decides on privilege references; nominates panel of vice-chairpersons; heads the RS secretariat; decides disqualification under the Tenth Schedule for RS members.','**Neutrality**: unlike the Speaker, not a member and cannot be removed as Chairman except by removal as VP (Art 67(b)); cannot preside when such a resolution is under consideration.','**Recent**: first-ever removal notice (Dec 2024) rejected; Jagdeep Dhankhar\'s resignation (July 2025); C.P. Radhakrishnan elected (Sep 2025).'],conclusion:'The Chairman\'s impartiality is central to the RS as a revising, federal chamber.',value:['**Art 64, 67, 89, 92**','Dec 2024 notice']},
'm2022-2':{intro:'**Art 329(b)** bars challenges to elections except by an election petition under law. **RPA 1951, Part VI** lays down the procedure.',body:['**Procedure**: petition in the **High Court** within **45 days** of the result (Sec 81), by a candidate or elector; tried by a single judge; to be concluded within **6 months** (Sec 86(7)); appeal to the **SC** within 30 days (Sec 116A).','**Grounds (Sec 100)**: the returned candidate was not qualified or was disqualified; **corrupt practice** by the candidate or agent (Sec 123); improper acceptance or rejection of nomination; improper reception or refusal of votes; non-compliance with the Constitution or RPA materially affecting the result.','**Case law**: *Indira Gandhi v Raj Narain (1975)*; *Abhiram Singh (2017)*: appeals to religion of anyone; *Lok Prahari (2018)*: non-disclosure of assets of associates as undue influence; *Ponnuswami (1952)*: no challenge mid-election.','**Problems**: delays (petitions outlast terms), costly, limited deterrence.'],conclusion:'Fast-track election benches and strict timelines are needed to give the remedy real meaning.',value:['**Ponnuswami (1952)**','**Abhiram Singh (2017)**','**Lok Prahari (2018)**']},
'm2022-3':{intro:'*K.S. Puttaswamy (2017)* held that privacy is intrinsic to life and liberty (Art 21) and other Part III freedoms.',body:['**Privacy dimensions**: bodily integrity, informational privacy, decisional autonomy (reproduction).','**DNA testing and paternity**: Evidence Act Sec 112 (now **BSA Sec 116**) gives a conclusive presumption of legitimacy for a child born during a valid marriage. Courts order DNA tests only where there is **eminent need** and a strong prima facie case (*Goutam Kundu, 1993*; *Bhabani Prasad Jena, 2010*; *Ashok Kumar v Raj Gupta, 2021*).','**Prenatal DNA testing**: invasive procedures (amniocentesis) carry risk; PCPNDT Act concerns; mother\'s bodily autonomy and the child\'s interest weigh heavily; courts are reluctant to compel.','**Balance**: best interests of the child, right to know parentage, and privacy.'],conclusion:'Privacy is not absolute, but compelled DNA testing, especially of a child in the womb, needs a high threshold and proportionality.',value:['**Puttaswamy (2017)**','**Bhabani Prasad Jena (2010)**','**Ashok Kumar (2021)**']},
'm2022-4':{intro:'**Art 213** lets the Governor promulgate ordinances when the legislature is not in session.',body:['**Conditions**: (1) Assembly (or both Houses) not in session; (2) Governor satisfied that circumstances require **immediate action**; (3) on **CoM advice**; (4) **President\'s instructions** needed if a Bill with the same provisions would need his previous sanction, would have to be reserved, or would be invalid without his assent; (5) must be laid before the legislature and lapses **6 weeks** after reassembly.','**Limits**: only on subjects within State competence; cannot violate FRs (Art 13); justiciable for mala fides (*Cooper*, *Krishna Kumar Singh*).','**Re-promulgation**: *D.C. Wadhwa (1987)*: Bihar re-promulgated ordinances for years; a "fraud on the Constitution". *Krishna Kumar Singh (2017, 7 judges)*: re-promulgation without placing before the legislature is a fraud; failure to lay is a serious breach; an ordinance does not automatically create enduring rights after lapse.'],conclusion:'Re-promulgation without legislative scrutiny is unconstitutional; ordinances must remain an emergency tool.',value:['**D.C. Wadhwa (1987)**','**Krishna Kumar Singh (2017)**']},
'm2022-5':{intro:'There is no explicit environmental right in Part III, but the SC read it into **Art 21**, combined with Arts **48A** and **51A(g)**.',body:['**Doctrines**: *M.C. Mehta (Oleum gas, 1987)*: **absolute liability**; *Vellore Citizens (1996)*: **precautionary** and **polluter pays** principles, sustainable development; *M.C. Mehta (Kamal Nath, 1997)*: **public trust doctrine**; *Subhash Kumar (1991)*: pollution-free water and air.','**Institutions**: continuing mandamus in *T.N. Godavarman (1995–)* on forests; CNG in Delhi; Taj Trapezium; **NGT (2010)** after SC push.','**Recent**: *M.K. Ranjitsinh (2024)*: right against adverse effects of climate change; Aravallis, stray firecrackers, air quality orders.','**Critique**: courts as administrators; implementation gaps; balance with livelihoods.'],conclusion:'Constitutionalising environment is a major achievement; durable outcomes need strong regulators and legislative follow-through.',value:['**M.C. Mehta**','**Vellore Citizens (1996)**','**Ranjitsinh (2024)**']},
'm2022-6':{intro:'India elects its President **indirectly**; France elects its President **directly** (since 1962).',body:['**India (Arts 54, 55)**: electoral college of elected MPs and elected MLAs (incl. Delhi, Puducherry); PR by single transferable vote; vote value formula (1971 census) ensuring uniformity among States and parity with the Union; secret ballot; disputes to the SC.','**France**: universal suffrage; two-round system (absolute majority needed; else top two in a run-off); 5-year term, max two consecutive terms; real executive powers (semi-presidential).','**Rationale**: India\'s President is nominal; direct election would create rival legitimacy to the PM. France\'s President leads executive policy; direct mandate fits.','**Critique**: India: vote value frozen, no nominated members; France: risk of cohabitation and polarised run-offs.'],conclusion:'Each method fits its system: a ceremonial head in a parliamentary democracy versus an empowered head in a semi-presidential one.',value:['**Art 54, 55**','French Constitution of 1958, 1962 referendum']},
'm2023-1':{intro:'Dicey: British Parliament can make or unmake any law; no body can override it. India follows **constitutional supremacy** with parliamentary government.',body:['**Britain**: unwritten constitution; no judicial review of Acts; Human Rights Act 1998 allows only **declarations of incompatibility**; devolution statutes; EU membership limited sovereignty (1972–2020); *Miller* cases show courts protecting parliamentary sovereignty itself.','**India**: written constitution; Parliament\'s power limited by FRs, federal division (Arts 245–246), judicial review (Arts 13, 32, 226) and the **basic structure** (*Kesavananda*); special amendment procedure.','**Similarities**: parliamentary executive, collective responsibility, privileges (Art 105 borrowed from the Commons).','**Contrast in practice**: India\'s judiciary strikes down amendments (NJAC 2015); the UK Supreme Court cannot strike down Acts.'],conclusion:'India rejected both British parliamentary sovereignty and American judicial supremacy, creating a balance that the basic structure doctrine protects.',value:['**Kesavananda (1973)**','**Minerva Mills (1980)**','**Miller (2017, 2019)**'],model:'**Introduction**\nParliamentary sovereignty in Britain, as A.V. Dicey defined it, means Parliament can make or unmake any law and no court can set it aside. India adopted the Westminster model of government but subordinated Parliament to a written Constitution.\n\n**The British approach**\n- No written constitution; Acts of Parliament are supreme.\n- Courts cannot invalidate statutes; under the Human Rights Act 1998 they can only issue declarations of incompatibility.\n- Limits are political (conventions, elections) and, until Brexit, EU law.\n\n**The Indian approach**\n- Constitutional supremacy: Parliament is a creature of the Constitution (Art 79).\n- Legislative competence limited by the Seventh Schedule; laws violating FRs are void (Art 13).\n- Amending power is limited by the basic structure (Kesavananda, 1973; Minerva Mills, 1980); NJAC struck down in 2015.\n\n**Convergence**\n- Both have a parliamentary executive and privileges; UK courts in Miller (2019) asserted limits on prorogation.\n\n**Conclusion**\nIndia blends parliamentary government with judicial review. This middle path, protected by the basic structure, prevents both legislative absolutism and judicial overreach.'},
'm2023-2':{intro:'Committees are of two kinds: **standing** (permanent: financial, DRSCs, inquiry, scrutiny, house-keeping) and **ad hoc** (select/joint committees on Bills, inquiry JPCs).',body:['**Structure**: financial committees (PAC 22, Estimates 30, COPU 22); 24 DRSCs (31 each); committees to inquire (Petitions, Privileges, Ethics); to scrutinise (Assurances, Subordinate Legislation, Papers Laid); welfare (SC/ST, Women); house-keeping; consultative committees.','**Financial committees and institutionalisation**: continuous scrutiny of spending (PAC on CAG reports), economy (Estimates), PSU efficiency (COPU); Action Taken Reports create a culture of answerability; bipartisanship (PAC chair from Opposition).','**Examples**: PAC on 2G and CWG; Estimates Committee on railway safety; COPU on Air India.','**Limits**: post-mortem, advisory, limited coverage, delayed CAG reports, lack of research staff.'],conclusion:'Financial committees have institutionalised legislative oversight of money. Timely reports, follow-up and a Parliamentary Budget Office would deepen this.',value:['PAC 2G report','**John Mathai**','**Krishna Menon Committee**']},
'm2023-3':{intro:'**Art 356** was used over 100 times by the early 1990s but sparingly afterwards.',body:['**Legal factors**: *S.R. Bommai (1994)*: proclamation is justiciable; floor test is the test of majority; Assembly should not be dissolved until Parliament approves; courts can restore governments. *Rameshwar Prasad (2006)*, *Nabam Rebia (2016)*, *Harish Rawat (2016)* reinforced this.','**Political factors**: coalition era (1989–2014): Centre depended on regional parties; Rajya Sabha approval needed; Sarkaria\'s "last resort" norm; public and media scrutiny; President K.R. Narayanan returned UP (1997) and Bihar (1998) recommendations.','**Institutional**: Governors\' reports under scrutiny; NCRWC and Punchhi recommendations.','**Recent uses**: J&K (2018–19), Puducherry (2021), **Manipur (2025)**: tied to breakdown situations rather than political dismissals.'],conclusion:'Bommai transformed Art 356 from a political weapon into a justiciable emergency power; coalition politics reinforced that restraint.',value:['**S.R. Bommai (1994)**','**Sarkaria**','Manipur 2025'],model:'**Introduction**\nArt 356 lets the President assume a State\'s functions when its government cannot be carried on according to the Constitution. Until 1994 it was imposed over 100 times, often to dismiss opposition governments.\n\n**Legal factors**\n- S.R. Bommai (1994, 9 judges): satisfaction is justiciable; floor test is the only test of majority; the Assembly cannot be dissolved before Parliament\'s approval.\n- Courts restored governments in Uttarakhand and Arunachal (2016).\n- Rameshwar Prasad (2006) struck down the Bihar dissolution.\n\n**Political factors**\n- Coalition governments depended on regional parties.\n- Approval needed in a Rajya Sabha where the Centre often lacked a majority.\n- Presidents returned recommendations (K.R. Narayanan, 1997–98).\n- Sarkaria Commission\'s "last resort" norm gained acceptance.\n\n**Present position**\nRecent impositions (J&K 2018, Manipur 2025) relate to genuine breakdowns.\n\n**Conclusion**\nThe combination of judicial review and political federalism has made Art 356 the exception Ambedkar intended.'},
'm2023-4':{intro:'The **101st Amendment (2016)** introduced GST from 1 July 2017.',body:['**Significance**: subsumed 17 taxes and 13 cesses; common national market; removed cascading; Art **246A** (concurrent power), **269A** (IGST), **279A** (GST Council); deleted Art 268A; widened tax base; GSTN.','**Accommodative federalism**: a pooled sovereignty; States participate equally in the GST Council (2/3 weight); 5-year compensation; consensus-based decisions (only one vote taken, 2019); *Mohit Minerals (2022)*: recommendations persuasive, not binding.','**Limits**: Centre\'s effective veto (1/3 weight + 3/4 threshold); loss of States\' fiscal autonomy; compensation ended June 2022; petroleum and alcohol excluded; dispute resolution mechanism not created; delays in GST dues.','**Recent**: GST 2.0 (2025): two main slabs.'],conclusion:'GST reflects co-operative federalism in design, but a fairer voting structure and a dispute-resolution mechanism would make it truly accommodative.',value:['**Mohit Minerals (2022)**','GST 2.0 (2025)','**Kelkar Task Force**']},
'm2023-5':{intro:'The Speaker (Arts 178–181) and Chairman (Arts 182–185) preside over State legislatures.',body:['**Maintaining order**: rulings on procedure; suspension of members; adjournments; naming members.','**Impartiality**: casting vote only in tie; decisions on Money Bills; **Tenth Schedule** adjudication.','**Concerns**: delayed or partisan defection decisions (Maharashtra 2022–23, Telangana 2024–25); mass suspensions; Speakers facing removal notices deciding disqualification (*Nabam Rebia*, referred to 7 judges).','**Judicial guidance**: *Kihoto Hollohan*: decisions justiciable; *Keisham Meghachandra (2020)*: decide within 3 months; *Subhash Desai (2023)*: political party appoints the whip; *Padi Kaushik Reddy (2025)*: time-bound decisions.','**Best practices**: All India Presiding Officers Conference norms; resignation from party; digital legislatures (NeVA).'],conclusion:'Presiding officers must be seen as neutral guardians of the House; time-bound and transparent decisions are essential.',value:['**Kihoto Hollohan**','**Subhash Desai (2023)**','**NeVA**']},
'm2023-6':{intro:'An independent judiciary ensures the rule of law, protects rights and arbitrates federal disputes: core conditions of democracy.',body:['**Constitutional guarantees**: security of tenure (Art 124(4)), salaries charged on the CFI (Art 125), no discussion of conduct (Art 121), contempt power (Art 129), ban on practice after retirement, Art 50.','**Judicial safeguards**: collegium (*Second and Third Judges*), NJAC struck (2015); basic structure.','**Threats**: delays in appointments; post-retirement appointments; transfers; executive non-compliance; vacancies; pressure from social media; questions about transparency.','**Accountability balance**: in-house procedure, removal by Parliament (Justice Varma case 2025), RTI on judges\' assets (*Subhash Chandra Agarwal, 2019*).'],conclusion:'Independence and accountability reinforce each other; both are needed for a judiciary to sustain democracy.',value:['**NJAC case (2015)**','**Indira Gandhi v Raj Narain**']},
'm2023-7':{intro:'**Art 39A** directs free legal aid; the **Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987** created NALSA (in force 1995).',body:['**Eligible (Sec 12)**: SC/ST members, victims of trafficking or begar, women and children, persons with disabilities, victims of mass disaster or violence, industrial workmen, persons in custody, those below an income ceiling.','**NALSA\'s role**: legal aid clinics, front offices; **Lok Adalats** and National Lok Adalats (crores of cases settled); **Legal Aid Defence Counsel System (2022)**; legal literacy; victim compensation schemes; undertrial review committees; Tele-Law with Ministry of Law.','**Gaps**: low awareness; quality of panel lawyers; low fees; undertrials remain ~75% of prisoners.'],conclusion:'NALSA has widened access, but quality and outreach must improve for Art 39A to be real.',value:['**Hussainara Khatoon (1979)**','**LADCS (2022)**','India Justice Report']},
'm2024-1':{intro:'A "living constitution" grows through interpretation. Art 21 is the clearest example.',body:['**From Gopalan to Maneka**: procedure must be just, fair and reasonable (1978).','**Expansion**: livelihood (*Olga Tellis*), speedy trial (*Hussainara*), legal aid, dignity, privacy (*Puttaswamy*), health, environment, die with dignity (*Common Cause*), sexual orientation (*Navtej*), climate change (*Ranjitsinh, 2024*), shelter against arbitrary demolition (2024), digital access (*Amar Jain, 2025*).','**Progressive society**: rights for transgender persons (*NALSA*), reproductive autonomy (2022).','**Limits**: no same-sex marriage (*Supriyo, 2023*); deference to legislature.'],conclusion:'Art 21\'s expansion shows a Constitution able to respond to new challenges while remaining anchored in dignity.',value:['**Maneka Gandhi (1978)**','**Puttaswamy (2017)**','**Ranjitsinh (2024)**'],model:'**Introduction**\nThe Constitution is a living document that adapts through amendment and interpretation. The right to life and personal liberty under Art 21 shows this best.\n\n**From narrow to expansive**\n- A.K. Gopalan (1950): any procedure established by law sufficed.\n- Maneka Gandhi (1978): procedure must be just, fair and reasonable; Arts 14, 19 and 21 form a golden triangle.\n\n**Expanding horizons**\n- Socio-economic: livelihood (Olga Tellis), shelter, education (Art 21A).\n- Justice: speedy trial, free legal aid, protection against custodial violence.\n- Autonomy: privacy (Puttaswamy, 2017), sexual orientation (Navtej, 2018), reproductive choice (2022), dignity in death (2018).\n- Environment: pollution-free air and water; protection against climate change (Ranjitsinh, 2024).\n- Digital: internet access as a medium (2020), accessible e-KYC (2025).\n\n**Limits**\nCourts have declined some claims (same-sex marriage, 2023), showing restraint.\n\n**Conclusion**\nArt 21 has become a reservoir of rights, making the Constitution responsive to a progressive society.'},
'm2024-2':{intro:'Electoral reforms have been recommended by many bodies since the 1970s.',body:['**Committees**: Tarkunde (1974), Dinesh Goswami (1990), Vohra (1993), Indrajit Gupta (1998), Law Commission 170th (1999) and 255th (2015), NCRWC (2002), 2nd ARC (2007), Kovind (2024).','**Implemented**: voting age 18, EVMs/VVPAT, affidavits, NOTA, open ballot in RS, expenditure limits, electoral bonds (struck down 2024).','**One Nation, One Election**: Kovind Committee: simultaneous LS + Assembly polls, then local bodies within 100 days; Art 82A; 129th Bill (Dec 2024) with a JPC. **For**: cost, governance, turnout. **Against**: federalism, accountability, logistics, voter behaviour.','**Other pending needs**: decriminalisation, party funding transparency, inner-party democracy, statutory MCC, regulation of social media and deepfakes.'],conclusion:'ONOE is one part of a wider agenda. Reforms that build trust and accountability matter as much as efficiency.',value:['**Kovind (2024)**','**ADR v Union (2024)**','**Law Commission 255th**']},
'm2024-3':{intro:'Centre–State relations have shifted in recent years across fiscal, administrative and legislative spheres.',body:['**Changes**: GST (2017) and its Council; rise of cesses and surcharges outside the divisible pool; 14th–16th FC devolution (42%/41%); NITI Aayog replacing the Planning Commission; centrally sponsored scheme restructuring (2016); borrowing limits (Kerala suit 2024); J&K reorganisation (2019); Delhi services law (2023); proposed IAS deputation changes (2022); Governor–State disputes; CBI general consent withdrawals; Online Gaming Act (2025); delimitation debate.','**Trust-building measures**: regular **Inter-State Council** meetings; consultation on Concurrent List laws; limit cesses or share them; GST dispute resolution mechanism; time-bound action by Governors; code for central agencies; Punchhi and Sarkaria recommendations; transparent FC terms of reference.'],conclusion:'Co-operative federalism needs institutions that resolve differences routinely, not through litigation.',value:['**Sarkaria**, **Punchhi**','**Presidential Reference (2025)**','**16th FC**']},
'm2024-4':{intro:'Arts 148–151 and the CAG (DPC) Act 1971 define the CAG\'s mandate.',body:['**Legality (regularity) audit**: whether expenditure was authorised by law and appropriation.','**Propriety audit**: whether it was wise, economical, faithful: guards against extravagance even if legal (e.g. excessive spending on events).','**Performance audit**: efficiency, economy, effectiveness of programmes (MGNREGA, PMAY, Bharatmala).','**Significance**: enforces executive accountability through the PAC; exposes waste and corruption (2G, coal).','**Limits**: post-facto; secret service expenditure; recommendations need parliamentary follow-up.'],conclusion:'The CAG\'s role extends beyond legality to propriety, making it a guardian of the public purse.',value:['**Arvind Gupta (2013)**','Ambedkar on CAG']},
'm2025-3':{intro:'The **J&K Reorganisation Act, 2019** made J&K a UT with a legislature under **Art 239A** (like Puducherry), effective 31 Oct 2019.',body:['**Nature**: unicameral Assembly of **90** elected members (2022 delimitation; 43 Jammu, 47 Kashmir, 9 ST seats) plus LG-nominated members; 24 PoJK seats vacant; 5-year term.','**Powers**: legislate on State List and Concurrent List matters **except public order and police**; Parliament\'s laws prevail; Bills affecting certain matters need LG\'s prior sanction.','**Executive**: CM and CoM (max 10% of Assembly); LG acts in discretion on police, public order, AIS, ACB; 2024 rules require LG approval for Advocate-General, prosecution sanctions, transfers.','**Functions**: budget, oversight, law-making; but limited fiscal autonomy and statehood demands (Cabinet resolution 2024).'],conclusion:'The Assembly restored representative government but with curtailed powers; statehood restoration remains the key constitutional question.',value:['**In Re Art 370 (2023)**','**Art 239A**','2024 business rules']},
'm2025-4':{intro:'**Art 76** creates the Attorney General, the highest law officer, appointed by the President during his pleasure.',body:['**Responsibilities**: advise the Government of India on legal matters; perform legal duties assigned; appear for the GoI in the SC and HCs; represent the GoI in Art 143 references.','**Rights**: audience in all courts; speak in Parliament and committees without vote (Art 88); MP\'s privileges (Art 105(4)).','**Limitations**: no advice or brief against the GoI; no defence of accused without permission; no company directorships without permission; not a full-time government servant (private practice allowed); not a Cabinet member.','**Issues**: tenure at pleasure; perceived proximity to the government; dual role as counsel and constitutional adviser.'],conclusion:'The AG\'s value lies in giving independent legal advice; security of tenure and conventions of independence would strengthen the office.',value:['**Art 76, 88, 105**','**M.C. Setalvad** (first AG)']},
'm2025-5':{intro:'**Arts 323A and 323B** (42nd Amendment) enabled specialised tribunals.',body:['**Need**: specialised expertise (tax, telecom, environment), speed, reduced HC burden, flexible procedure.','**Problems**: executive control over appointments; vacancies; tenure insecurity; tribunals as retirement havens; *L. Chandra Kumar (1997)*: HC review retained.','**Tribunals Reforms Act, 2021**: abolished 9 appellate bodies (FCAT, IPAB, AAI tribunal etc.) moving cases to HCs; uniform search-cum-selection committees; reintroduced 4-year tenure and 50-year minimum age, which *MBA (2021)* struck down again.','**Impact**: some rationalisation, but cases shifted to HCs; conflict with judiciary continued; **2026 Act** created a National Tribunals Commission.'],conclusion:'Tribunals add value only if they are independent and well-resourced; an autonomous oversight body is the right direction.',value:['**L. Chandra Kumar (1997)**','**MBA (2021)**','**Tribunals Reforms Act 2026**']},
'm2025-6':{intro:'**Art 368** gives Parliament constituent power, subject to procedural and substantive limits.',body:['**Procedural limits**: Bill only in Parliament; special majority in each House separately; no joint sitting; ratification by half the States for federal provisions; President must assent. The **131st Bill (2026)** failed for want of the two-thirds limb.','**Substantive limits**: basic structure (*Kesavananda*); limited amending power itself basic (*Minerva Mills*); Ninth Schedule laws after 1973 reviewable (*I.R. Coelho*); NJAC struck (2015).','**Debate**: legitimacy of judge-made limits vs protection of constitutional identity.'],conclusion:'Procedural rigour and substantive limits together keep amendments deliberative and the Constitution\'s core intact.',value:['**Kesavananda (1973)**','**Minerva Mills (1980)**','131st Bill (2026)']},
'm2025-7':{intro:'Centre–State finance moved from plan-based transfers to formula-based and pooled-tax systems.',body:['**Planning era (1951–2014)**: Planning Commission\'s plan grants (Art 282) alongside FC transfers; Gadgil formula (1969); centralised allocation.','**Reforms**: Planning Commission replaced by **NITI Aayog** (2015); **14th FC** raised devolution to **42%** and merged plan/non-plan; **GST** (2017) pooled indirect taxes; restructured CSS (2016); FRBM limits; 15th FC (41%) with performance grants; 16th FC (41%, GDP contribution).','**Effects on fiscal federalism**: more untied funds but reduced taxation autonomy; rising cesses and surcharges; GST compensation ended 2022; borrowing restrictions; conditional grants.'],conclusion:'Reforms increased formula-based devolution but also centralised tax power; restoring trust needs predictable, untied resources for States.',value:['**14th, 15th, 16th FCs**','**Mohit Minerals (2022)**']},
'm2025-1':{intro:'**Sec 123(2) RPA** defines undue influence as any direct or indirect interference with the free exercise of electoral right.',body:['**Disproportionate assets**: not per se "undue influence"; they may indicate corruption (PC Act) but undue influence needs interference with voters.','**Lok Prahari v Union (2018)**: non-disclosure of assets and sources of income of candidates and their associates can amount to undue influence; directed disclosure of sources of income; monitoring mechanism for unexplained asset growth.','**Disclosure regime**: Sec 33A affidavits (*ADR 2002*, *PUCL 2003*); Form 26.','**Gaps**: no permanent mechanism to verify asset growth; CBDT role limited.'],conclusion:'Unexplained asset growth threatens electoral integrity; a statutory monitoring mechanism, as Lok Prahari urged, is needed.',value:['**Lok Prahari (2018)**','**ADR (2002)**']},
'm2025-2':{intro:'Both Presidents can pardon, but on different constitutional logics.',body:['**India (Art 72)**: pardon, commutation, remission, respite, reprieve for court-martial, Union-law offences and death sentences; exercised on **CoM advice** (*Maru Ram*); limited judicial review (*Kehar Singh*, *Epuru Sudhakar*); delays can lead to commutation (*Shatrughan Chauhan*); cannot be pre-emptive in practice (needs conviction).','**USA (Art II, Sec 2)**: personal discretion; only federal offences; not for impeachment; can be **pre-emptive** (before charge or conviction): Ford–Nixon (1974); pardons of family and officials in Jan 2025; self-pardon untested.','**Pre-emptive pardon**: forgiveness before charges are filed, shielding from future prosecution.','**Limits**: India: Constitution, advice, review; USA: political accountability only.'],conclusion:'India\'s model embeds pardon within responsible government and judicial review, reducing the scope for abuse seen with pre-emptive US pardons.',value:['**Maru Ram (1980)**','**Kehar Singh (1989)**','**Ex parte Garland (1866)**'],model:'**Introduction**\nThe power of pardon tempers justice with mercy. India\'s President exercises it under Art 72; the US President under Art II, Sec 2.\n\n**Comparison**\n- Scope: India covers court-martial, Union-law offences and death sentences; the US covers federal offences except impeachment.\n- Advice: India\'s President acts on Council of Ministers\' advice (Maru Ram, 1980); the US President acts personally.\n- Review: limited judicial review in India for arbitrariness or mala fides (Kehar Singh, 1989; Epuru Sudhakar, 2006); in the US almost none.\n- Timing: India grants after conviction; the US allows pardons before charges.\n\n**Pre-emptive pardons**\nA pre-emptive pardon forgives a possible offence before any charge (e.g. Ford\'s pardon of Nixon, 1974; pardons in January 2025). Critics say it can shield allies from accountability.\n\n**Limits**\nIndia: constitutional advice, judicial review, delays as ground for commutation. US: political accountability and public opinion.\n\n**Conclusion**\nIndia\'s design ties mercy to responsible government, making the power less prone to personal misuse.'},
'm2019-1':{model:'**Introduction**\nArt 368 empowers Parliament to amend the Constitution. Whether this extends to destroying its basic features was settled by the Supreme Court.\n\n**Evolution**\n- Golaknath (1967): Parliament cannot abridge FRs.\n- 24th Amendment (1971): Parliament can amend any part.\n- Kesavananda Bharati (1973): Parliament can amend any part, but cannot alter the basic structure.\n\n**Attempt to expand power**\n- 42nd Amendment (1976) inserted Art 368(4) and (5): no limitation on amending power and no judicial review.\n- Minerva Mills (1980) struck these down: a limited amending power is itself a basic feature; Parliament cannot convert a limited power into an unlimited one.\n\n**Subsequent affirmation**\n- Waman Rao (1981), I.R. Coelho (2007), NJAC (2015).\n\n**Conclusion**\nParliament cannot destroy the basic structure, nor enlarge its power to do so. The doctrine preserves constitutional identity against temporary majorities.'},
});
