'm2014-5':{body:['**Constitutional cap**: 91st Amendment limits the CoM to 15% of the LS (max 81); earlier ministries exceeded 70–80.','**Large cabinets**: coordination failures, overlapping ministries, slower decisions, coalition bargaining (UPA had 70+), cost; but more representation of regions and groups.','**Small cabinets**: quicker decisions, clearer accountability, but overloaded ministers and concentration in the PMO.','**What really drives efficacy**: rational allocation of business (2nd ARC: merge ministries into ~20–25 departments), cabinet committees, competent ministers, coordination by the Cabinet Secretariat, delegation to secretaries.','**Evidence**: 2014 "minimum government, maximum governance" merged ministries (e.g. Overseas Indian Affairs into MEA); outcomes depended on delivery systems more than headcount.']},
'm2015-3':{body:['**Right**: Art 21 includes a pollution-free environment (*Subhash Kumar, 1991*); Arts 48A and 51A(g); precautionary principle (*Vellore Citizens*).','**Health impact**: PM2.5 spikes several times above safe limits after Diwali in Delhi NCR; noise pollution; vulnerable groups.','**SC orders**: *Arjun Gopal (2017, 2018)*: sale ban in NCR (2017), then only **green crackers**, fixed time windows, no joint/series crackers; later reaffirmed (2021) that bans apply nationwide in polluted areas.','**Competing rights**: Art 25 (religious practice: crackers not essential), Art 19(1)(g) (livelihood of Sivakasi workers); proportionality requires least restrictive measures.','**Implementation**: enforcement weak; need for awareness and alternatives.']},
'm2016-1':{body:['**Sovereign**: independent internally and externally; membership of the UN, WTO, Commonwealth is voluntary; still defensible amid globalisation.','**Socialist** (1976): democratic socialism, mixed economy, welfare State; post-1991 liberalisation shifted means, not ends (DPSPs, food security, MGNREGA → VB-G RAM G); upheld in *Dr Balram Singh (2024)*.','**Secular** (1976): equal respect, State neutrality, minority rights; basic structure (*Bommai*); challenged by communal polarisation.','**Democratic**: political, social, economic democracy; universal franchise; challenged by money, criminalisation, weak inner-party democracy.','**Republic**: elected head of State; all offices open to all citizens.']},
'm2016-3':{body:['**Essentials (69th Amdt, 1991)**: NCT; 70-member Assembly; CoM (max 10%); State and Concurrent subjects except public order, police, land; LG to act on aid and advice; differences referred to the President (Art 239AA(4)); Art 239AB failure of machinery.','**Anomalies**: "any matter" proviso; control of services unclear; ACB jurisdiction; dual control of DDA (land) and police; LG\'s discretion vs elected mandate.','**Conflicts**: 2015–2018 standoffs over appointments, files, Mohalla clinics; SC (2018, 2023) rulings; GNCTD (Amendment) Acts 2021 and 2023 (NCCSA).','**New trend?** Asymmetric federalism disputes spread to Governor–State conflicts (TN, Kerala, Punjab); litigation as politics; need for conventions.']},
'm2018-1':{body:['**Ground (Art 360)**: President satisfied that the financial stability or credit of India or any part is threatened.','**Approval**: both Houses within **2 months**, simple majority; continues **indefinitely** until revoked; no repeated approval; judicial review possible (44th Amendment removed finality).','**Consequences**: Centre can direct States on canons of financial propriety; reduce salaries and allowances of State employees; reserve Money and Financial Bills for the President; reduce salaries of Union employees including **SC and HC judges**.','**Never invoked**, not even in 1991; criticised in the CA (H.N. Kunzru) as a threat to State autonomy.']},
'm2019-1':{body:['**Kesavananda (1973)**: Parliament can amend any part, not the basic structure.','**42nd Amendment (1976)**: Art 368(4),(5) attempted unlimited power and barred judicial review.','**Minerva Mills (1980)**: struck down 368(4),(5); limited amending power is a basic feature; balance between FRs and DPSPs.','**Later**: *Waman Rao (1981)*, *I.R. Coelho (2007)* on the Ninth Schedule; NJAC (2015) shows the doctrine in action.']},
'm2020-3':{body:['**UK convention**: Speaker resigns from party; parties do not contest the Speaker\'s seat; re-elected unopposed; seen as above politics.','**Indian need**: Speakers decide Tenth Schedule disqualifications, Money Bill certification and admission of motions; partisanship and delays (*Keisham Meghachandra 2020*: suggested independent tribunal).','**Implications**: greater neutrality and trust; continuity of procedure; but the Speaker\'s constituency loses an active representative; needs cross-party consensus; Indian parties may not reciprocate.','**Alternatives**: transfer defection adjudication to the ECI or a tribunal; fixed timelines; resignation from party on election.']},
'm2020-4':{body:['**Co-operation**: GST Council (only one vote in 50+ meetings); COVID vaccination drive; NITI Governing Council; Zonal Councils; disaster response.','**Competition**: NITI indices (SDG, Export Preparedness, Health); Ease of Doing Business; investor summits; Aspirational Districts rankings.','**Confrontation**: Governors withholding assent (TN, Kerala, Punjab → 2023 and 2025 SC rulings); CBI consent withdrawals; ED actions; GST compensation dues; borrowing limits (Kerala suit); Farm Acts (2020); NEET; deployment of forces; delimitation fears.','**Result**: federalism is negotiated; institutions decide whether contestation becomes conflict.']},
'm2020-7':{body:['**Current procedure**: HC finds corrupt practice in an election petition → President decides disqualification period (up to 6 years) on the **ECI\'s opinion** (Sec 8A); appeals lengthen the process.','**Problems**: election petitions take years (Sec 86(7)\'s 6-month limit is rarely met); disqualification often comes after the term ends; little deterrence.','**Simplification**: special election benches; automatic disqualification on HC finding subject to stay; fixed timelines for the President\'s decision; ECI empowered to act directly; Law Commission 244th/255th suggestions.']},
'm2021-1':{body:['**Meaning**: commitment to the Constitution\'s core values (liberty, equality, fraternity, dignity) and its processes; contrasts with popular or societal morality.','**Origin**: Ambedkar (4 Nov 1948, quoting Grote): constitutional morality is not natural; it must be cultivated.','**Judicial use**: *Manoj Narula (2014)*: PM\'s discretion in appointing ministers; *Navtej Johar (2018)*: Sec 377; *Sabarimala (2018)*; *Joseph Shine (2018)*; *GNCTD (2018)*: collaborative federalism.','**Critique**: vague; can enlarge judicial discretion (Justice Indu Malhotra\'s dissent in Sabarimala on essential practices); balance with democratic choices.']},
'm2021-2':{body:['**USA**: 14th Amendment equal protection; formal, colour-blind approach in recent cases; affirmative action in college admissions struck down (*SFFA v Harvard, 2023*); strict scrutiny of racial classification.','**India**: Art 14 (equality before law and equal protection), reasonable classification and non-arbitrariness; Arts 15(3)–(6), 16(4), 16(4A) enable **substantive** equality; Art 17 abolishes untouchability; Art 46 DPSP.','**Doctrines**: creamy layer (*Indra Sawhney*), 50% ceiling, EWS (*Janhit Abhiyan*), sub-classification (*Davinder Singh 2024*).','**Horizontal application**: India extends some equality rights against private persons (Arts 15(2), 17); US State-action doctrine is narrower.']},
